{%- comment -%}
  The free delivery threshold in integer cents, from the one setting that defines it.
  Every other use of the threshold goes through this: free-delivery-amount renders it
  for display, and the cart page and bag drawer size their progress bars from it.

  Why the setting is text and not a number. Shopify's `number` setting type cannot hold
  a decimal: it silently rounded 54.99 to 55, so the whole site read "free delivery over
  €55" while the policy and the Shopify discount both said €54.99. A text setting can
  hold it; the cost is that a merchant can type anything, which is what this snippet is
  for. It was typed `number` until 1 Oct 2026 precisely because, as text, "€65" used to
  evaluate to zero — so the sanitising below is not optional, it is the reason the type
  change is safe.

  remove '€' and ',' and strip: "€54.99" and " 54.99 " both parse. Use a point for the
  decimal; a comma decimal ("54,99") would read as 5499 and is called out in the
  setting's own help text.

  times: 100 then round: 54.99 * 100 is 5498.999999999999 in floating point, and the
  live money filter rounds that to the whole euro. Rounding here makes the cents
  integral before anything formats or divides by them.

  at_least: 100 is load-bearing twice over. The cart page divides by this value, so 0
  would throw. And it is 100 rather than 1 because a 1-cent threshold means free
  delivery on every order — a typo that gives away delivery is worse than one that
  throws, so the floor is €1, which is what the number setting's at_least: 1 meant.

  Usage: {%- capture fd_cents -%}{%- render 'free-delivery-cents' -%}{%- endcapture -%}
         {%- assign threshold = fd_cents | plus: 0 -%}
{%- endcomment -%}
{{- settings.free_shipping_threshold | remove: '€' | remove: ',' | strip | times: 100 | round | at_least: 100 -}}
